(Findings store: one directory per finding, self-contained enough that
 anyone can reproduce it without vox-fuzz.)

a text called findings_dir is "./findings".
a number called findings_count is 0.

(Which layout the recorded program was written in, so a reader of a
 finding knows whether program.vox's whitespace was drawn or plain - and
 so a campaign that reproduces the finding can ask for the same mode.
 The program.vox in the finding is a byte copy of the file that actually
 compiled and ran, so repro.sh reproduces byte-for-byte whatever this
 says; the line is metadata for the human, not an instruction to the
 script. Mirrored from gen_core.vox's gen_layout by loop_gen.vox, which
 is the only place a finding is created from a generated program - this
 file is included by tests that do not load the generator at all, so it
 cannot read that global directly.)
a text called findings_layout is "random".

(The harness input surface a recorded program ran under, so repro.sh can
 replay it. Like findings_layout these are mirrored out of the generator
 by loop_gen.vox - the one place a finding is created from a generated
 program - because this file is included by tests that do not load the
 generator and cannot see gen_argv/gen_scratch_argv directly. findings_argv
 is the drawn flags BEFORE the scratch pair is prepended (loop_gen.vox
 captures it at that point), so repro.sh can pass the program the same
 flags without the dead pid+seed scratch path the harness swept.
 findings_scratch_flag is the alias the program declared for its scratch
 directory, or "" when it did no file I/O. Both stay empty under the unit
 tests, so a test-built repro.sh runs ./prog with no args, exactly as
 before. Env is not among these: the generator does not choose it
 (gen_misc.vox), and printing raw env values would break the privacy rule
 this store already follows, so env is inherited by repro.sh, not replayed.)
a list called findings_argv is [].
a text called findings_scratch_flag is "".

To 'finding path' with a text called category and a number called seed.
    a text called p is "{findings_dir}/{category}/{seed}".
    Return a text, p.

To 'finding exists' with a text called category and a number called seed.
    a text called 'the finding directory' is 'finding path' of category and seed.
    Return a boolean, 'the finding directory' is available.

(finding path/category/seed never carry attacker- or flag-controlled text
 - only findings_dir does (--out) - but the whole directory is built and
 checked as one text value, so there is nothing left to quote here: 'is
 available' is access(2), not a shell.)

(Everything below that is plain filesystem work - making directories,
 copying a file, writing text - is done in Vox, not through /bin/sh: per
 plan 322 A2, that is the fix, not quoting, wherever the language can do
 the work itself. findings_dir/vox_binary/vox_core (--out/--vox/--core)
 are user-controlled; a shell that spliced them in raw is exactly finding
 02. Only two steps are left on a shell at all - capturing `vox --version`
 and setting the executable bit - because Vox has no way to capture a
 child's stdout or to chmod a file. Both interpolate vox_binary and 'the
 finding directory' only through 'shell quote' (harness.vox), so there is
 one place to audit for every value that started outside this source.
 repro.sh's own vox_binary line is quoted the same way for a different
 reason: that script is not a shell command vox-fuzz runs, but a spaced
 --vox would still leave whoever runs repro.sh later with a broken
 script - the benign half of finding 02 again, one step removed.)

To 'finding save' with a text called category and a number called seed and a text called src and a text called detail.
    a text called 'the finding directory' is 'finding path' of category and seed.
    a boolean called 'already saved' is 'finding exists' of category and seed.
    If 'already saved' then,
        Set findings_count to findings_count add 1,
        Return a number, findings_count.
    a number called 'the mkdir result' is 'make directories' of 'the finding directory'.
    open a file for reading called 'the program source' at src.
    a buffer called 'the program bytes'.
    Read from 'the program source' into 'the program bytes'.
    Close 'the program source'.
    (PROBE g2: empty program.vox is the symptom of defect 15 - the source
     was unreadable when finding save ran. Warn loudly so a forced run
     shows it on stdout instead of hiding in the findings dir.)
    If 'the program bytes's size is 0 then,
        Print "PROBE g2: EMPTY program.vox for {category} seed {seed} src {src}".
    a text called 'the program copy path' is "{'the finding directory'}/program.vox".
    open a file for writing called 'the program copy' at 'the program copy path'.
    Write 'the program bytes' to 'the program copy'.
    Close 'the program copy'.
    a text called 'the classification path' is "{'the finding directory'}/classification.txt".
    open a file for writing called 'the classification file' at 'the classification path'.
    Write "category: {category}\nseed: {seed}\nlayout: {findings_layout}\ndetail: {detail}\n" to 'the classification file'.
    Close 'the classification file'.
    a text called 'the version command' is "{'shell quote' of vox_binary} --version >> {'shell quote' of 'the finding directory'}/classification.txt 2>&1".
    a number called 'the version result' is 'run shell' of 'the version command' and 10000.
    a text called 'the repro path' is "{'the finding directory'}/repro.sh".
    (Build the command line the harness used, so repro.sh runs the
     program under the same input surface it had when the defect fired:
     the drawn flags, and when the program did file I/O a fresh scratch
     directory under the same alias - the harness's pid+seed scratch was
     swept after the run, so repro.sh makes its own and traps it for
     cleanup. Each drawn arg is shell-quoted; the scratch path is a shell
     variable, so it never enters this Vox text unquoted. Env is inherited
     by repro.sh, not replayed - the generator does not choose it, and
     printing raw env values would break the privacy rule this store
     already follows; stdin is not replayed either, and most programs take
     none. Both gaps are noted here so a reader who needs them knows.)
    a text called 'the repro args' is "".
    If findings_scratch_flag is not "" then,
        Set 'the repro args' to " {'shell quote' of findings_scratch_flag} \"$REPRO_SCRATCH\"".
    For each 'the drawn arg' in findings_argv,
        Set 'the repro args' to "{'the repro args'} {'shell quote' of 'the drawn arg'}".
    a text called 'the scratch setup' is "".
    If findings_scratch_flag is not "" then,
        Set 'the scratch setup' to "REPRO_SCRATCH=\"$(mktemp -d)\" || exit 1\ntrap 'rm -rf \"$REPRO_SCRATCH\"' EXIT\n".
    open a file for writing called 'the repro file' at 'the repro path'.
    Write "#!/bin/sh\nexport VOX_CORE_PATH=\"${{VOX_CORE_PATH:-{vox_core}}}\"\n{'the scratch setup'}{'shell quote' of vox_binary} program.vox -o prog && ./prog{'the repro args'}\necho \"exit: $?\"\n" to 'the repro file'.
    Close 'the repro file'.
    a text called 'the chmod command' is "chmod +x {'shell quote' of 'the finding directory'}/repro.sh".
    a number called 'the chmod result' is 'run shell' of 'the chmod command' and 5000.
    Set findings_count to findings_count add 1.
    Return a number, findings_count.

(`ver` is an alternate spelling of the reserved keyword `version`, which
 is why the version line's local carries the full phrase.)